    "body": "<p>The night soundscape is a different world from the day&#x27;s. Birds largely fall silent, and the biophony passes to owls, nightjars, frogs, crickets, and katydids, with mammals, from bats to foxes to deer, adding rustles, barks, and screams. Geophony changes too: wind often drops after sunset, rain sounds sharper against quiet, and distant sounds carry further in the cool, still night air. Anthrophony thins in rural places but persists in cities, where traffic and nightlife hold the night soundscape at a higher level. Night listening rewards patience: a listener who sits still for an hour hears the soundscape&#x27;s structure, the owl&#x27;s territory, the frog chorus&#x27;s pulse, the insect band&#x27;s steady hum, in a way no daytime visit reveals. Night soundscapes are also the most vulnerable to light and noise pollution, both of which shift animal activity away from the dark hours.</p><h4>Science proof</h4><p>Science proof: night acoustic monitoring documents the distinct nocturnal community, including owl territories, frog choruses, and insect bands, and shows that night recordings detect species missed by day surveys. Studies of light and noise pollution show shifted calling times and reduced nocturnal activity in affected areas. The physics of night sound propagation, cooler, calmer air carrying sound further, is standard atmospheric acoustics.</p><h4>Math proof</h4><p>Math proof: if a night recording holds 45 minutes of cricket sound, 20 minutes of frog calls, and 15 minutes of owl calls in a 120-minute window, with 10 minutes of overlap between crickets and frogs, the total biophony time is 45 + 20 + 15 - 10 = 70 minutes, a share of 70 / 120 = 58.3 percent. The demo prints total 70 minutes and share 58.3 percent, plus the silent remainder, 120 - 70 = 50 minutes.</p><h4>Soundscape layer(s)</h4><p>biophony; geophony</p><h4>Source</h4><p>Published nocturnal bioacoustics literature; atmospheric acoustics references, as published October 2026</p>",
